# SFML — Software Factory Markup Language

> SFML describes a software factory: a durable, resumable pipeline graph that turns an
> intent into a reviewed artifact. This file tells an LLM how to read the spec, use the
> example tool, and write and validate a factory.

## Read this first

- Full spec: https://sfml.craftmill.dev/spec/ (source: SPEC.md in the repo)
- Repo: https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml

## Writing a factory

- A factory is a YAML/JSON document. SPEC.md clause 5 defines the document format
  (encoding, naming, unknown/reserved keys); clauses 6-7 define the data model (top-level
  fields, step types, connections, the expression language, and the values a running
  factory exposes to expressions and prompt templates).
- SPEC.md clause 8 defines the static validity rules a linter must enforce — read it before
  assuming a graph is well-formed.
- `sfml.schema.json` at the repo root is the JSON Schema (2020-12) for a factory document's
  surface syntax (SPEC Annex A). It catches structural mistakes; it does not check the
  graph-level rules of clause 8.
- `example/` is a worked example implementation (SPEC Annex C), not the spec. Don't infer
  normative behavior from it — SPEC.md is normative.

## Testing a factory

Clone the repo and install both the root and example dependencies:

    git clone https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml
    cd sfml
    npm install && npm install --prefix example

Then:

- Validate against the schema: `node tools/validate-schema.mjs path/to/factory.sfml`
- Lint with the example tool (checks clause 8 graph validity, not just schema shape):
  `npm run lint:factory`
- Run the example's own conformance/unit tests for reference: `npm run test:example`
- Run everything the repo's CI runs: `npm test`

## Remember

The example tool in `example/` is one implementation, not the spec. If its behavior and
SPEC.md ever disagree, SPEC.md wins.
